-- title:
--   The choice graph is a subgraph of Definition 5.13's favored graph
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a configuration, $W$ a choice function assigning a double cone $W(B)$ to
--   every set $B$, and $Q, P$ two blocks. Suppose $W(Q)$ is favored by majority in $Q$ and
--   $W(P)$ is favored by majority in $P$. Then adjacency in the **choice graph** — the graph in
--   which $Q$ and $P$ are joined when $Q \ne P$ and either $y \in W(Q)[x]$ for all $x \in Q$,
--   $y \in P$, or the same with the roles reversed — implies adjacency in the undirected
--   **favored graph** of Definition 5.13, whose edge relation asks only that *some* cone favored
--   by majority in the source block do the job.
--
--   The content is entirely in the direction of the implication. Remark 5.12 of
--   Bux–Kassmann–Schulze observes that the majority cone of a block is in general not unique, so
--   the favored graph's per-edge existential is genuinely weaker than fixing one cone per block
--   in advance. This lemma is what makes the two comparable: connectivity in the choice graph
--   (`QFS.renormalization_choice`) implies connectivity in the favored graph, which is what
--   Proposition 5.14 asserts (`QFS.renormalization`).
--
--   The choice graph does not appear in the source. It was introduced here to carry a later step
--   of Theorem 5.15's construction, where one lattice representative per block has to serve both
--   of the block's incident edges, and that fails if consecutive edges may pick different
--   majority cones.
